{
 "description": "When a PR's pull_request closed and main-push webhooks are lost (aerial#2327 merged as 353b1b05 during the 2026-11-21 08:34Z NATS outage), legacy synapse still self-heals: the deploy path ResolveSHA misses pr_registry, resolves the commit through the GitHub commits API and BackfillPushMergeSHA flips the row to merged on that commit. PRObject never heard about the merge, so under authority it stayed open forever (no deploy tracking, no deploy_success). Fix: when a deploy/rollout/main commit SHA resolves to a PR only through the GitHub fallback and GitHub reports that PR merged with merge commit == the SHA, synapse sends PRObject.HandleMerged (merge SHA = the commit, head = the PR head GitHub reports) before forwarding the deploy event, keyed merge-recovery:repo#pr@merge, unless PRObject already merged on that SHA or does not track the PR. A first merge applies on its merge SHA even when PRObject's head is stale (a missed synchronize) and adopts the GitHub head unless it is in PriorHeads. lookup is the synapse side: GitHub commits/pulls answer and PRObject GetDeployView; recovery_shadow/recovery_restate are the HandleMerged sends synapse makes for a legacy and a restate-owned event. prstate replays setup + recovery + deploy in both modes: merged -> deploying -> deployed, DeployedJobs equal the row, the projection agrees with the row, RepoObject resolves the merge SHA, ListRollouts is empty, and the legacy intended effects (the merge's flux_reconcile on the merge commit, which a normal merge runs too, and deploy_success) equal the restate-owned published ones. Contrast: the late real closed webhook and main push for the same merge afterwards change nothing and run no effects. Case 1: synchronize and CI success reached PRObject. Case 2: the synchronize was lost too, so PRObject's head is stale and the recovered merge adopts the GitHub head.",
